224609375, -0.006603240966796875, 0.0002923011779785156, -0.018524169921875, -0.0030040740966796875, 0.01544952392578125, -0.00376129150390625, 0.0009665489196777344, -0.00876617431640625, 0.015716552734375, 0.023651123046875, 0.0543212890625, 0.0025768280029296875, 0.0235443115234375, -0.0390625, -0.0191802978515625, 0.022552490234375, -0.00707244873046875, 0.01507568359375, 0.01273345947265625, -0.033966064453125, -0.030975341796875, 0.005428314208984375, 0.0037937164306640625, 0.00839996337890625, 0.0255279541015625, -0.01284027099609375, -0.004116058349609375, 0.01611328125, 0.006832122802734375, -0.00235748291015625, 0.01412200927734375, 0.0132904052734375, -0.005481719970703125, -0.037200927734375, -0.0084228515625, -0.0190582275390625, 0.00528717041015625, 0.0128936767578125, -0.003326416015625, 0.0308685302734375, -0.025543212890625, -0.0295562744140625, 0.006561279296875, 0.00745391845703125, 0.021209716796875, -0.045379638671875, -0.0034732818603515625, 0.0085601806640625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence redistributes execution and introduces a governance challenge: determining where human direction of the task persists. We distinguish two approaches. Specified delegation situates human direction before execution, through instructions, constraints, or criteria that define the task. Iterative coproduction situates it during execution, through interventions that correct or redirect provisional outputs. To analyze both approaches, we use aggregate monthly data from the Anthropic Economic Index for April and May 2026. The AEI differentiates two usage modes: 1P API, representing direct traffic through Anthropic's API, and Claude.ai, which aggregates activity from Chat and Cowork. Based on this, we test whether a stronger work-related orientation in human-AI interaction is associated with increased specified delegation within each mode, and whether the rise in the iterative profile is greater in Claude.ai than in 1P API. The main analysis uses level-0 O*NET tasks and estimates how both profiles shift when an eligible record reallocates ten percentage points from personal to work-related use. The iterative comparison is limited to 1,411 node-month pairs observed and eligible in both modes. Specified delegation increases by 2.76 points in 1P API (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) and by 1.45 in Claude.ai (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]). On the common support, iterative coproduction changes by -0.30 points in 1P API and by 0.15 in Claude.ai, resulting in a between-mode difference of 0.45 points (95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]). These results indicate that a work-related orientation is associated with stronger evidence of prior human direction, and that the observable iterative response differs across usage modes. The article shifts focus from the extent of AI execution to the timing of observable human direction."}}